 "drainage": {
  "_comment": "Rivers and lakes (#72), cut in the bake, never at runtime. TODO(initiator): every value is a starting point. Rain is relative: rain_base + rain_moisture_gain * moisture. A river starts where the rain-weighted catchment reaches river_min_catchment_km2; river_depth_m and river_width_m are [at the start, at most]; river_fill is the share of the bed that holds water. A sink at least lake_min_depth_m deep, and lake_min_area_m2 large or fed by a river, holds a lake; rivers cut through shallower ones. sea_min_area_km2: areas below the sea level count as the sea for the water from this size on; smaller pockets fill to a lake or a river crosses them (0: every one is sea). river_sinks: how a river crosses a sink without a lake, cut (its bed cuts the sill down to the sink's floor) or fill (sediment up to the spill point, the river runs across). lake_evaporation: rain-weighted catchment a lake needs per m\u00b2 of surface; a sink that gathers less holds a smaller lake that never spills, or stays dry. Hearth (playtest #72: few long rivers from the heights to the sea, fewer small lakes): only seas from 0.5 km\u00b2 count as sea, sinks under 50 m on a river fill with sediment, only deep ones keep a lake. Erosion: stream power on the macro grid, per step strength * catchment^area_exponent / distance, at most max_m in all.",
